-### Neural Radiance Caching
-An application where an MLP predicts photorealistic pixel colors from feature buffers independently for each pixel. The feature buffers contain world-space position $x$ and additional features. It is supervised online during real-time rendering using sparse light paths spawned from the camera view [S5, unofficial]. The world-space position $x$ is encoded using multiresolution hash encoding and concatenated with additional auxiliary encoded dimensions before feeding into the MLP [S5, unofficial]. Performance at 1920x1080px resolution is 133 FPS with multiresolution hash encoding vs 147 FPS with triangle wave encoding, with an overhead of 0.7 ms including training and runtime costs [S5, unofficial]. Hash collisions cause microstructure artifacts at the finest grid resolution, which do not disappear with longer training times [S5, unofficial]. Training budget is one millisecond per frame to support animated content [S5, unofficial].
+### MLCA (Hybrid Spatial Feature Learning Module)
+A module added after position encoding and before the MLP to capture spatial feature information and mix channels of these features, establishing mixed associations in feature dimension space [S5]. It processes the concatenated position-encoded spatial position $x$ and direction $d$ ($X_{input} = Concat(PE(x), PE(d))$) through two fully connected layers with GELU activation: $X_{token} = GELU(W_{token} X_{input} + b_{token})$ and $X_{channel} = GELU(W_{channel} X_{token} + b_{channel})$ [S5].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of MLP layers and the input dimension `in_mlpC`. The positional encoding adds a constant factor overhead per sample.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Dominated by the MLP weights ($O(in\_mlpC \cdot featureC + featureC^2)$) and the positional encoding buffers if cached.
-*   **Performance:** In Neural Radiance Caching, multiresolution hash encoding achieves 133 FPS at 1920x1080px, compared to 147 FPS for triangle wave encoding [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Captures high-frequency details through positional encoding; view-dependent effects via view direction encoding.
-*   **Limitations:** Susceptible to overfitting if not regularized or if the network is too small for the scene complexity [S3]. Hash collisions can cause artifacts in caching applications [S5, unofficial]. No-position proposer fails on non-forward-facing scenes [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of sample points $N$ and the input dimension $in\_mlpC$. The positional encoding step is also linear in $N$ and the number of frequency bands.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Linear with respect to $N$ for storing intermediate activations. The model parameters are fixed based on `featureC` and `inChanel`.
+*   **Strengths:** Simple architecture, differentiable, effective for learning view-dependent effects when combined with positional encoding [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Can overfit on small datasets without regularization or multi-input structures [S3]. Standard positional encoding may struggle with high-frequency details compared to learned or hybrid approaches [S4, unofficial][S5].
+*   **When to use:** Standard neural volume rendering where view-dependent effects are important and computational resources are limited.
+*   **When not to use:** When high-frequency detail recovery is critical and overfitting is a concern (consider [MLP with Per-layer Inputs Incorporation](4051-mlp-with-per-layer-inputs-incorporation.md) or [MLCA](4947-mlca-hybrid-spatial-feature-learning-module.md)).
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from the foundational work on Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) [S1, S2, S4, unofficial], which introduced the use of MLPs with positional encoding for implicit neural representations of 3D scenes. The specific implementation details, such as the concatenation order and bias initialization, are derived from various open-source implementations and analyses [S1, S2, unofficial]. Variants like MLP with Per-layer Inputs Incorporation were proposed to address overfitting in few-shot view synthesis [S3]. Neural Radiance Caching applies this concept to real-time rendering with hash encoding [S5, unofficial].
+Originated in the context of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) [S1, S2, unofficial]. The use of positional encoding for MLPs in neural rendering was popularized by [NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis](1576-neural-volume-rendering.md) [S2, S4, unofficial]. The specific implementation details described here are derived from common implementations in the community [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
